[TESTING FOR SYNTAX]

abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ <
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token 'newline'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ < <<
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token '<<'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << <
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token '<'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << |
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token '|'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ < a |
> hello
    bash: a: No such file or directory
    Command 'hello' not found, but can be installed with:
    snap install hello              # version 2.10, or
    apt  install hello              # version 2.10-2ubuntu4
    apt  install hello-traditional  # version 2.10-5
    See 'snap info hello' for additional versions.

Bash is referencing tokens even during single line inputs.
Apparently splits the current line into tokens before getting a full line, and
before checking for redirections and so on.

->  Tokenizer runs on each line. If syntax error is found during tokenization
    return an error. 

->  We return the Tokens and also store the whoel string in history.

abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << a<
    > a
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token 'newline'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << a| <
    > a
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token 'newline'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << a>
    > a
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token 'newline'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << a>>
    > a
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token 'newline'
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ << a <<b
    > a
    > b

checks for << redirection and gets input before <, >, >> redirections

[TOKENS]

Tokens hold the split string and the type of the string.

Tokens need to be split by 'space', 'newline', '|', '' '<', '<<', '>', '>>'.
Tokens need to be split by qoutes. Every other potential split character needs
to be ignored while in qoutes.

abronner@c3a7c1:~$ <<<< EOF
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token `<'
abronner@c3a7c1:~$ <<<<< EOF
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token `<<'
abronner@c3a7c1:~$ <<<<<< EOF
    bash: syntax error near unexpected token `<<<'

As soon as bash reaches the maximum length of a non string token it stores it
without checking the next characters.

	For examples check the example_tokens.c file.

Examples of how string need to be split:

abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ cat<<a<Makefile -e|cat -e>out
    > Hello
    > a
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$

line:   >   "cat<<a<Makefile -e|cat -e>out"
        >   "Hello World"
        >   "a"

    line gets split into multiple strings.

str:    "cat" "<<" "a" "<" "Makefile" "-e" "|" "cat" "-e" ">" "out" "\n" "Hello World" "\n" "NULL"

type:   

line:   >   "cat<<a<Makefile -e|(grep "fclean"&&cat -e>out1||cat >out2 -e)"
        >   "Hello World"
        >   "a"

str:    "cat" "<<" "a" "<" "Makefile" "-e" "|" "(" "grep" "fclean" "&&" "cat" "-e" ">" "out1" "||" "cat" ">" "out2" "-e" ")" "\n" "Hello World" "\n" "NULL"

type:   

Example for a potential token struct:

typedef struct s_tokens
{
    char    *str;
    char    **split;
    int     *type;
}   t_tokens;

[TYPES OF TOKENS]

[TYPE]      [DESC]

string      any string of non syntax related characters or characters enclosed by quotes

pipe        pipe symbol '|'

and         control operator and "&&"

or          control operator or "||"

open        control operator open "("

close       control operator close ")"

<           redirection in

<<          redirection here document

Can be stored in a list and later on be transformed into a split.

Readline will read a line. Token and syntax check the single line.
During contruction do syntax check on each token (check if the just created
token is a valid follow for the previous one (?)).
    If a invalid token is created print error and delete tokens, but return
    string to be put into history.
If an unfinished token exists we need to read further (qoutes).

[EXPORT && ECHO]

variables in a heredoc don't get expanded, they just get deleted.

abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ export HELLO=WORLD
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ echo $HELLO
    WORLD
abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$ echo << EOF
    > $HELLO
    > EOF

abronner@c3a7c1:~/projects/minishell/tests$

